WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 11: The Nika Riots: The Empress Who Saved an Empire

1
00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:05.120
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:06.320
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:12.800
[Rebecca] Today we witness one of the most violent, explosive, and cinematic urban uprisings in the ancient world.

4
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:20.080
[Rebecca] In the 6th century, Constantinople was the wealthiest metropolis on Earth, governed by Emperor Justinian the Great.

5
00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:25.120
[Rebecca] Yet, Imperial politics was driven by an obsession with chariot racing.

6
00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:34.880
[Rebecca] Two rival sports factions, the Blues and the Greens, functioned like organized crime syndicates, political parties, and urban militias rolled into one.

7
00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:43.120
[Rebecca] In January of 532, these bitter rivals put aside their blood feuds and united under a single terrifying battle cry.

8
00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:45.440
[Rebecca] Mika, meaning victory.

9
00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.000
[Rebecca] Within 48 hours, the capital was reduced to smoking embers.

10
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:59.200
[Rebecca] The emperor was huddled on the docks ready to flee into exile, and an empire was saved by a former street actress who refused to surrender her crown.

11
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.760
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story.

12
00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:08.160
[George] In 6th century Constantinople, the Hippodrome was far more than a sporting arena.

13
00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:12.560
[George] It was the pulsating, violent political heart of the Roman Empire.

14
00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:22.160
[George] Seating over 100,000 screaming citizens, this colossal marble stadium was physically connected to the great palace by a private imperial walkway.

15
00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:30.240
[George] The populace of the capital was fanatically divided into factions named after their chariot racing teams, the blues and the greens.

16
00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:33.120
[George] These factions were not merely sports fans.

17
00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:38.480
[George] They operated as armed political parties, neighborhood gangs, and private militias.

18
00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:49.600
[George] They controlled protection rackets, extorted waterfront merchants, dominated civic pageantry, and exerted enormous influence over the appointment of senior imperial magistrates.

19
00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:57.600
[George] In early January of 532 AD, public anger reached an unprecedented boiling point against Emperor Justinian.

20
00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:08.600
[George] Justinian was pursuing an aggressive, expensive military campaign to reconquer the Western Roman Empire from the barbarian kingdoms, funding his legions through crushing taxation.

21
00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:24.760
[George] Public resentment focused squarely upon two deeply despised imperial ministers, Tribonian, the arrogant legal architect, and John the Cappadocian, the ruthless Praetorian prefect who used torture and extortion to squeeze every copper coin from the citizenry.

22
00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:35.000
[George] When violent factional brawling erupted in the narrow streets following a race, resulting in several murders, the city prefect decided to make an example of the hooligans.

23
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:43.640
[George] Seven ringleaders from both the blues and the greens were arrested, convicted, and sentenced to hang in the public square across the Golden Horn.

24
00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:48.680
[George] During the execution, the wooden gallows collapsed twice under the weight of the condemned.

25
00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:59.800
[George] A sympathetic crowd surged forward, rescuing the final two surviving prisoners, one blue and one green, and smuggling them across the water to sanctuary inside the church of St.

26
00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:00.760
[George] Canaan.

27
00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:08.680
[George] On Tuesday, January 13, at the races in the Hippodrome, a tense, volatile silence fell over the stands.

28
00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:19.160
[George] For twenty one consecutive races, the crowd chanted respectful petitions to Justinian, begging the emperor sitting in the royal Cathysma box to pardon the two condemned men.

29
00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:25.160
[George] Justinian sat impassive, draped in imperial silk, completely ignoring the pleas.

30
00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:29.400
[George] At the conclusion of the 22nd race, the unimaginable occurred.

31
00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:33.960
[George] The blues and the greens ceased hurling insults and stones at one another.

32
00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:43.560
[George] United by a shared burning hatred of imperial despotism, one hundred thousand spectators rose to their feet and began roaring in terrifying unison.

33
00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:47.920
[George] Nika, Nika, Nika, win, win, win.

34
00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:53.520
[George] The cry of Nika transformed a sports crowd into a revolutionary army.

35
00:03:53.520 --> 00:04:03.280
[George] Pouring out of the massive arched gates of the Hippodrome in an uncontrollable, rampaging torrent, the mob stormed the Praetorium, the headquarters of the city prefect.

36
00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:12.400
[George] They overwhelmed the garrison, massacred the guards, released every prisoner from the public dungeons, and set the administrative buildings ablaze.

37
00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:21.200
[George] A ferocious winter wind blowing off the Black Sea seized the sparks, driving the flames across the historic civic center of Constantinople.

38
00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:25.360
[George] For five agonizing days, the greatest city on earth burned.

39
00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:35.520
[George] The historic cathedral of Hagisophia, originally built by Constantine the Great, collapsed into a glowing mountain of charred cedar beams and shattered marble.

40
00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:47.760
[George] The Senate House, the magnificent public baths of Zexipus, ancient libraries housing classical manuscripts, and hundreds of aristocratic palaces along the central Mies thoroughfare were incinerated.

41
00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:57.760
[George] The sky over the Bosporus was blotted out by suffocating clouds of soot, while burning embers drifted miles across the Golden Horn to set ships afire in the harbor.

42
00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:08.800
[George] Armed with butcher cleavers, iron crowbars, and captured military weapons, armed gangs hunted down imperial tax collectors and magistrates, lynching them in the streets.

43
00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:18.240
[George] Justinian attempted to appease the mob by dismissing Tribonian and John the Cappadocian from their offices, but his concessions were rejected with contempt.

44
00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:31.240
[George] On Sunday morning, January 18, seeking to dismantle the dynasty completely, the mob marched to the residence of Flavius Hypatius, the aristocratic nephew of the former emperor Anastasius.

45
00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:41.400
[George] Despite Hypatius's wife weeping and clutching his knees, begging him not to accept what amounted to a death sentence, the mob carried Hypatius to the Hippodrome.

46
00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:51.160
[George] They placed a heavy golden necklace upon his head as a makeshift diadem, lifted him onto the imperial podium, and proclaimed him the new emperor of the Roman world.

47
00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:58.200
[George] Inside the fortified perimeter of the great palace, Emperor Justinian was paralyzed by overwhelming despair.

48
00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:06.600
[George] The palace complex was completely surrounded by tens of thousands of armed rebels hurling stones, torches, and insults at the iron gates.

49
00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:16.200
[George] The imperial palace guards, demoralized by the sheer size of the uprising and divided in their loyalties, refused to advance into the burning streets.

50
00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:21.400
[George] Justinian convened an emergency council of state in the private imperial dining hall.

51
00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:35.320
[George] Gathered around the table were his most seasoned military commanders, including General Belisarius, who had recently returned from the Persian border, General Mundus, commander of the Illyrian mercenaries and senior ministers.

52
00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:38.200
[George] The council was gripped by blind panic.

53
00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:43.080
[George] The imperial treasurer and the magistrates unanimously urged immediate flight.

54
00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:53.400
[George] Down at the base of the palace cliff, within the secluded private imperial harbor of Baucholian, three fast imperial dramans had already been prepared under cover of darkness.

55
00:06:53.400 --> 00:07:03.080
[George] Slaves had loaded the ships with heavy, ironbound chests containing millions of gold solidi, imperial regalia, and priceless religious relics.

56
00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:13.320
[George] The sails were hoisted, the oars were manned by trusted sailors, and the vessels were positioned to slip out onto the Sea of Marmara the moment Justinian gave the command.

57
00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:20.240
[George] The plan was to flee to the fortress city of Heraclea in Thrace, abandoning the capital to the usurper Hypatius.

58
00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:27.920
[George] Justinian sat head in hands, prepared to surrender his throne and end his reign in cowardly, humiliating flight.

59
00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:33.280
[George] And then, breaking the silence of the chamber, Empress Theodora rose to speak.

60
00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:38.640
[George] Empress Theodora was unlike any sovereign who had ever sat upon the throne of the Caesars.

61
00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:46.720
[George] The daughter of Acacius, a humble bear keeper in the Hippodrome, Theodora had grown up in the impoverished alleys of Constantinople.

62
00:07:46.720 --> 00:08:00.720
[George] In her youth, she had earned a living as a comedic stage actress, an acrobatic mime, and a dancer in waterfront taverns before Justinian had become utterly captivated by her fierce intellect, beauty, and indomitable will.

63
00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:07.120
[George] She understood the brutal realities of power far better than the aristocratic senators who surrounded her husband.

64
00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:16.800
[George] As the male counselors finalized their escape plans, Theodora stepped forward into the center of the hall, her jewelled robes rustling against the marble floor.

65
00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:25.920
[George] According to the contemporary court historian Procopius, she fixed her piercing gaze upon Justinian and delivered words that would echo across centuries.

66
00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:32.560
[George] Whether or not a woman should give an example of courage to men is of no concern, Theodora spoke with icy.

67
00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:34.400
[George] Unwavering conviction.

68
00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:37.760
[George] When danger is mortal, counsel must be bold.

69
00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:43.280
[George] For my part, I hold that flight, even if it brings safety, is utterly dishonorable.

70
00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:50.640
[George] Every man born into this world must die, but for one who has once reigned, to become a wandering fugitive is unendurable.

71
00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:54.240
[George] If you wish to save your life, Caesar, it is easy.

72
00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:59.120
[George] Your money is ready, the sea is open, and your ships are waiting at the quay.

73
00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:07.320
[George] As for me, I will never take off this royal robe, and I pray I may never live to see the day when those who meet me do not salute me as empress.

74
00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:12.520
[George] I hold to the ancient saying, royal purple makes the finest burial shroud.

75
00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:16.200
[George] Theodora's words struck the council like a thunderbolt.

76
00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:25.400
[George] Stung to the core by the fearless defiance of a former street performer, Justinian dismissed the escape ships and commanded his generals to prepare for battle.

77
00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:35.000
[George] General Belisarius and General Mundus immediately took command of the counteroffensive, coordinating their strike with the eunuch Grand Chamberlain, Narsus.

78
00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:38.280
[George] Narsus was a master of psychological warfare.

79
00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:49.400
[George] Slipping quietly out of a secret palace postern gate carrying heavy sacks of gold coin, Narsus entered the crowded hippodrome, seeking out the senior chieftains of the blue faction.

80
00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:55.400
[George] Pulling them into the shadowed porticos beneath the stands, Narses distributed handfuls of gold.

81
00:09:55.400 --> 00:10:08.680
[George] Whispering a stark reminder, Justinian had always been a loyal patron of the blues, whereas the usurper Hypatius was a sworn green sympathizer who would inevitably strip the blues of their wealth and municipal privileges.

82
00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:11.720
[George] The political wedge was devastatingly effective.

83
00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:25.000
[George] As Hypatius sat nervously on the imperial throne in the center of the arena, surrounded by cheering greens, thousands of blue partisans began quietly slipping out through the side arches, abandoning the coronation.

84
00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:30.920
[George] At that exact moment, Belisarius and Mundus struck with total, merciless precision.

85
00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:39.080
[George] Elite legionaries slammed shut every massive timber gateway and vomitorium of the Hippodrome, barricading the exits from the outside.

86
00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:46.960
[George] General Belisarius, drawing his broadsword, charged through the palace gate at the head of his battle-hardened Gothic heavy infantry.

87
00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:55.440
[George] Simultaneously, General Mundus burst through the gate of the dead with his ferocious Herulian mercenary cavalry, lances lowered.

88
00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:58.720
[George] The trap was sprung, and there was no escape.

89
00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:05.840
[George] What followed inside the marble oval of the Hippodrome was not a battle, but a systematic, industrial slaughter.

90
00:11:05.840 --> 00:11:14.080
[George] Trapped within the enclosed stadium with nowhere to run, the crowd was driven into a panic-stricken mass against the lower tiers of the arena.

91
00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:24.400
[George] Belisarius's veteran soldiers, hardened by years of brutal trench combat against the Sassanian Persians, cut through the screaming mob with mechanical efficiency.

92
00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:34.320
[George] By the time the winter sun set over the smoking ruins of Constantinople, over thirty thousand citizens lay dead upon the blood-drenched sand and marble benches.

93
00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:42.160
[George] The usurper Hypatius and his brother were dragged from the throne, weeping and pleading that they had been coerced by the mob at knifepoint.

94
00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:50.400
[George] Justinian was inclined to show mercy, but Theodora intervened, insisting that their survival was an intolerable threat to the crown.

95
00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:58.320
[George] The following morning, Hypatius was executed in the palace courtyard, and his body was cast into the depths of the Sea of Marmara.

96
00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:02.720
[George] The Nica riots were broken, and Justinian's throne was absolute.

97
00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:11.360
[George] From the charred ashes of the leveled metropolis, Justinian and Theodora launched the most ambitious architectural program in human history.

98
00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:25.120
[George] On the very site of the destroyed cathedral, Justinian commissioned the construction of a new Hagia Sophia, a colossal masterpiece of soaring brick domes and shimmering golden mosaics that remained the largest cathedral on earth for a millennium.

99
00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:35.640
[George] When the emperor stepped into the completed sanctuary in 537 AD, he gazed upward into the radiant dome and cried out, Solomon, I have surpassed thee.

100
00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:47.720
[George] Yet, every marble pillar of that golden marvel stood because of the iron resolve of Empress Theodora, who refused to run, proving that royal purple makes the finest burial shroud.

101
00:12:48.040 --> 00:12:52.680
[Rebecca] The Nica riots prove that courage is not a matter of birth, but of character.

102
00:12:52.680 --> 00:13:03.000
[Rebecca] Without Theodora's fearless refusal to run, Justinian's reign would have ended in cowardly exile, and Roman law, art, and architecture would have been fundamentally extinguished.

103
00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:05.960
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

104
00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:07.160
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

105
00:13:07.160 --> 00:13:10.520
[Rebecca] Join us next time for another forgotten chapter of history.

106
00:13:10.520 --> 00:13:13.000
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

